# OpenAI Docs

Provide current, cited OpenAI product, API, model, and Codex guidance. Read zero or one primary reference.

**First substantive action:** Search the user's exact requested official OpenAI documentation topic and any explicitly named model using a concise, topic-specific query of 2-6 essential terms. When an already-available direct official documentation search and page-retrieval capability is present, use it first: search, then fetch or open the matching official page before general web search. Otherwise, immediately use official-domain web search, then actually open or fetch the relevant official page. Complete this source order before reading a reference, inspecting local or repository files, running a Codex manual or model resolver, drafting a plan, or answering from memory. Use the actual fetched page, not a search snippet or an unopened link. If one official search or page does not establish the answer, search another appropriate official domain and actually open or fetch the result. Preserve the exact requested model; never substitute a newer model.

**Only exception:** An explicitly requested, genuinely broad, cross-topic Codex setup, orientation, or system-map synthesis may use the manual first when shell execution and an allowed temporary cache are available. A specific Codex feature, setting, command, error, model, or requested citation remains docs-first. Mixed Chat/Work/Codex comparisons are official documentation questions, not manual-first Codex requests.

For generic software tasks, answer the software task directly. OpenAI implementation, debugging, SDK, API, prompting, agent, and eval requests are not generic.

For a straightforward factual or citation-only request, follow the source order and do not read a route reference. This includes straightforward API facts, ChatGPT Work or mixed Chat/Work/Codex comparisons, model tiers, aliases, Pro mode, reasoning settings, factual migration baselines, and narrow Codex facts. Prioritize `learn.chatgpt.com` for ChatGPT Work.

## Choose one primary route

Use the first matching route, and read its reference only when the requested task needs that specialized workflow:

- **Explicitly requested local documentation integration:** Read [integration guidance](references/mcp-diagnostics.md) only when the user explicitly requests that local integration.
- **Model migration, upgrades, or model-specific prompting:** Read [model-migration.md](references/model-migration.md) for actual migration planning, implementation, dynamic target resolution, or prompt changes. Preserve an explicitly requested target.
- **Model selection and comparisons:** Read [model-selection.md](references/model-selection.md) only when nuanced current, latest, default, cost, latency, quality, or modality tradeoffs need more guidance. Do not run a migration resolver for selection alone.
- **Product, API, ChatGPT Work, and mixed Chat/Work/Codex documentation:** Read [official-docs.md](references/official-docs.md) only when fetched official pages leave source selection, API schemas, or the requested implementation unresolved. This route is not manual-first.
- **Explicitly broad Codex setup, orientation, or cross-topic synthesis:** Read [codex-self-knowledge.md](references/codex-self-knowledge.md) when the eligible Codex manual or deeper Codex procedures are needed.

Read at most one primary reference. Do not open every route, bundled model guide, or helper script. Read a supporting reference or run a helper only when the chosen workflow demonstrably needs it.

## Source and execution boundaries

- Search, open, fetch, and cite only `developers.openai.com`, `platform.openai.com`, and `learn.chatgpt.com`. Cite the page that supports the claim. State uncertainty when official sources do not establish pricing, availability, account access, limits, or behavior.
- Preserve an explicitly requested model for selection, migration, and prompting. Resolve an unspecified latest or current migration target only after searching and fetching current official guidance.
- Use `references/latest-model.md` only as a disclosed fallback after current official model guidance does not answer the question. Read `references/upgrading-to-gpt-6-astra.md` only for an actual, requested GPT-6 migration; read `references/prompting-guide.md` only for requested prompting work.
- Before building, running, editing, debugging, or testing an API-backed app or tool, use `openai-platform-api-key` first when available. Documentation, conceptual examples, model selection, and read-only guidance do not require an API key.
- Say "OpenAI Docs" or "official OpenAI documentation" in user-facing answers. Keep exact official citations and examples concise.
     1	# D172: isolated concurrency POC, first slice
     2	
     3	Alex accepted the D171 recommendation and asked to flag ordering or production contradictions immediately. This slice implements the offline coordinator and tests. It does not enable concurrent ingestion by default or launch paid calls.
     4	
     5	The controlling rule is unchanged: an item's accepted extraction must be a response to the exact request built from the committed preceding items. Speculative calls may run early; their answers are not handovers until their turn and validation. This deliberately changes the experimental call schedule, not committed knowledge. D159's default no-later-call-on-failure test continues to hold on the default path. In the experimental path, earlier failure cancels pending work and drains already running requests without accepting later handovers or writes.
     6	
     7	## Shape and ownership
     8	
     9	- `poc/concurrency_study/runner.py`: opt-in callable day runner, bounded extraction lookahead, request validation, immutable worker inputs, manifest and cleanup. Invoked through `replay.run(day_runner=...)`; default remains serial. The POC uses a single coordinator/writer and does not claim support for other processes writing this ledger.
    10	- `model.request_identity`: one exact serialization/hash function shared by normal calls, Jev calls and validation. Same bytes and keys as before.
    11	- `extraction.request_for`: one owner for extraction setting, complete prompt and schema; both extraction and validation use it.
    12	- `round.run(extractor=..., matching_workers=...)`: dependency injection for the POC; the existing checks, two matching phases, writer, completion records, failure handling and daily judging remain the owners.
    13	- `jev_matching.decide(workers=1)`: plan independent questions on the coordinator, send only immutable state/questions to threads, collect answers and mutate candidates/totals in original order. The default remains serial. A refused answer fails the item; successful concurrent responses remain archived for retry.
    14	- The Jev coordinator submits at most the worker count at a time. It checks all completed requests for failure before filling open slots; on failure it submits no more work, cancels what has not started, and drains running requests. Completion order never controls output order.
    15	- Identical Jev requests within a batch share one future, keyed by the complete request hash. Every logical consumer reads that same answer and archive ID. Later uses are accounted like cache hits, preserving the existing logical request order and aggregate hash. No second network answer may claim the first answer's call ID.
    16	- Every new Jev matching record includes the ordered individual call IDs and request hashes in `shown_json.requests`, alongside the existing candidate/evidence lists. Existing readers select those list keys; legacy records remain readable without the new metadata. This makes concurrent matching traceable without inferring call ownership from arrival order.
    17	- Persistent POC manifest: ordered input identities and source digests, sequence numbers, configuration, universe and code/prompt digests per day. Resume must match. A changed source/configuration is refused before speculative submission. Interrupted manifest creation refuses with a clear error rather than guessing.
    18	
    19	No writable thesis connection is passed to a worker. No worker writes handovers, memberships, matching records or completion markers. Archive access already has a lock; the network runs outside it. Local IDs remain scoped by input because the writer still handles one item at a time.
    20	
    21	## State × event
    22	
    23	| State | Event | Transition and write |
    24	|---|---|---|
    25	| Before day | Same manifest on resume | Reuse; default dispatch retains original sequences |
    26	| Before day | Changed/corrupt manifest | Refuse before launching calls; no source or ledger rewrite |
    27	| Prepared | Worker available | Submit bounded extraction with copied item/context and frozen configuration/universe |
    28	| In flight | Later result returns first | Archive only; never an accepted handover |
    29	| In flight | Failure | Retain exception until this request's turn; if stale, it does not invalidate a different current request |
    30	| Item's turn | Same complete request and frozen semantics | Accept its prepared handover through the normal round |
    31	| Item's turn | Changed request/schema/context | Cancel if pending; otherwise drain and keep the old archive record. Re-extract with current context; never remap old IDs |
    32	| Item's turn | Changed configuration/universe/source | Refuse the run; do not combine contracts |
    33	| Matching | Independent questions | Read on coordinator, run API calls concurrently, apply results in planned order |
    34	| Matching | Identical complete request appears twice | One in-flight future and archive result; both consumers receive the same data/ID, with only one fresh-call cost |
    35	| Matching | One question fails | Drain remaining calls; fail the item without a write; resume can reuse completed archived requests |
    36	| Writing | Exception | Existing savepoint rollback and failed-step record; no later accepted item |
    37	| Earlier item fails | Later speculative calls exist | Cancel queued calls, drain running calls, archive only; do not mark later items extracted or written |
    38	| Restart | Saved accepted handover exists | Use normal resume; do not speculatively re-extract it |
    39	| End of day | All items committed | Existing dependency-ordered judging and pull, then day completion |
    40	| End of day | Any failure | Existing day remains incomplete |
    41	
    42	Dates in accepted records remain source publication days; ordering remains dispatch sequence. Wall time describes calls only.
    43	
    44	## Value × reader and old checks
    45	
    46	| Value | Writers/readers | Proof |
    47	|---|---|---|
    48	| Exact request JSON/hash | Model/Jev, validation, archives | Existing hash bytes unchanged; schema included |
    49	| Prepared handover | Worker → coordinator → normal round | Used only for the same item, source, universe, settings and current request |
    50	| Jev result | Worker → coordinator | Completion order cannot change candidate decisions, evidence decisions, group refs, call aggregation or request hash |
    51	| Individual Jev call IDs/hashes | Jev coordinator → matching record → preflight/trace/recall readers | Ordered request metadata is additive; existing list readers retain candidate/evidence keys |
    52	| Per-day manifest | POC coordinator → restart | Exact source/configuration/order match; no silent config or source replacement |
    53	| Final IDs and writes | Existing writer → all readers | Identical fixture ledger to serial control after metadata/call IDs are removed |
    54	| Failure/retry | Existing round and replay | No later authoritative progress; completed prefix reused on restart |
    55	
    56	Tests use real temporary databases and real stored source material where available; only model/provider responses are faked. Cases: independent overlap; schema-only invalidation; same-day new thesis; changed group membership; failed earlier call; out-of-order completion; accepted-handover resume; writer failure; no later-day work; Jev response ordering and partial failure/cache reuse; manifest mismatch. Positive controls and scratch mutants must fail for stale-answer acceptance and completion-order mutation. The default sequential tests remain unchanged.
    57	
    58	The first read-only design review found one serious missing row: concurrent identical Jev calls can return different sampled answers but share the archive's winning call ID. The complete-request shared-future rule above closes this row before implementation. The network remains outside the archive's SQLite lock.
    59	
    60	The first code review found a second failure dimension: submitting all Jev jobs up front allowed queued jobs to start after another request failed. The bounded submission rule above closes it. A ten-job/two-worker control holds one request in flight, fails another and checks that no remaining request starts. Day cost is read from the archive delta, including discarded speculative calls; accepted-item costs alone undercount the new path.
    61	
    62	## Limits before a live POC
    63	
    64	The existing spend check runs after an item and is not a reservation for concurrent requests. Therefore this first POC entry uses explicit offline transports in tests. Before any live concurrent run, account for in-flight worst-case cost and timed-out unknown usage, and register the comparison's cap. Existing completed-run budgets are not reused. This is a live-launch prerequisite, not a claim that concurrency has been measured.
    65	
    66	The coordinator is single-writer, as the current POC is. It does not make the production system safe for uncoordinated external writers. No such guarantee is claimed; that would require a separate ownership/transaction design.
    67	
    68	## Result
    69	
    70	The offline implementation is complete. [Results and evidence](../../../poc/reports/2026-10-25-concurrency-poc/results.md): 143 tests passed, five scratch mutants caught, and 3,595 historical request identities unchanged. The focused follow-up review found no serious issue after the duplicate-request and queued-work corrections. Live accounting and a fresh performance/quality comparison remain outstanding.
    68	
    69	Speed is not progress: code written before the thinking costs rounds of review. A design holds these tables before code
    70	and before any review; a blank cell means it is not ready.
    71	
    72	1. **State × event**, for work that spans more than one step, run or day. Every state against every event it can
    73	   meet: success, refusal, a refused retry, an external failure, a crash midway, work still in flight when a run ends,
    74	   a later run on a later date, configuration changed in between, an input corrected while waiting, another writer of
    75	   the same data, two scopes at once. Each cell names the transition, what is written, and the date every read and
    76	   write uses. The rows include the work before its first step and after its terminal outcome.
    77	2. **Value × reader.** Every value a change writes, against every reader of it (found by searching the field) and
    78	   every input source the value can come from. A rule over a kind of data lists every writer of that kind. A change to
    79	   how often something fires is counted against every reader of its output.
    80	3. **New path × old checks.** For a new input, derived value, mode or changed data shape: every check the old path
    81	   passes, and whether the new one meets it. A changed shape re-derives every check written for the old one.
    82	4. **Prior decisions.** The decisions searched for the mechanism by name; any decision that rejected or bounded it is
    83	   quoted in the design and in the question to Alex.
    84	
    85	- A contract over text or shapes states the closed set of what passes (a full match); anything else fails or is not
    86	  judged. Enumerate the accepted set against every context and reader it meets before the first edit.
    87	- A blind review measures the tables. A serious finding they do not hold shows a missing dimension: add it and walk
    88	  every row again; never answer the finding alone. Code starts when the review finds nothing serious.
    89	- A design whose core contract is still open is a question, not something to build.
    90	- A foundation's acceptance carries a size and speed budget from its first control, not only correctness.
    91	
    92	## 3. Writing code
    93	
    94	- **One owner.** A rule lives where the data is written, and refuses: a warning that lets the run continue is a silent
    95	  failure. Fix the writer and delete its duplicate implementations in the same change; when a fix lands in one
    96	  consumer, search every consumer of the same key or rule.
    97	- **Code over prose.** A contract that code or a schema can enforce (an enum over allowed keys, a shape) is enforced
    98	  there, and stays out of prompts and documents.
    99	- **One word per concept.** The glossary in `docs/prd/00-system.md` (section 用词, D36) names each concept once, and
   100	  `docs/check.py` fails on a word it rules out. A new concept gets its row and its ruled-out words in the same change;

